{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "314",
    "case_summary": "A 2-year-old girl presented with skin fragility since early infancy. She had 2 older brothers, and the family had no medical history of note. Symptoms improved in winter and worsened in summer. Physical and mental developments were normal. The mother reported finding the child peeling off skin with marked facility.\n\nPhysical examination revealed:- Mild xerosis with superficial skin erosions- Erythematous residual macules from previous erosions- Some lesions had unusual linear geographic contour- Lesions predominantly located at areas of friction- Hair, nails, and mucous membranes were normal- No history of blistering\n\n1. Laboratory Tests:- Serological markers for celiac disease were negative2. Skin Biopsy Findings:- Hematoxylin-eosin staining showed:  * Complete separation of the stratum corneum  * Clean split just above the stratum granulosum  * Faint psoriasiform hyperplasia  * Mild perivascular and interstitial mononuclear infiltrate in upper dermis3. Immunofluorescence Mapping:- Normal results for:  * Collagen IV and VII  * α6 and β4 integrines  * Laminine-3324. Genetic Study:- Revealed 2 heterozygous mutations in exon 2 of the CDSN geneImages:- Figure A Title: Clinical photograph of disease outbreak  Description: Superficial erosions distributed predominantly around the trunk, some with geometric shape- Figure B Title: Photograph showing localized affectation  Description: Erythemato-desquamative well-demarcated plaque with peeling borders on right foot- Figure C Title: Hematoxylin-eosin staining  Description: Detachment of entire stratum corneum and mild psoriasiform epidermis hyperplasia",
    "root": {
      "label": "Chronic Progressive Skin Fragility Syndrome with Seasonal Variation and Normal Development",
